{
  "concept": "In any oblique triangle the altitude h from the apex gives the height used in area and sine-rule formulas, but when the angle at α is obtuse that altitude falls outside the triangle onto an extension of the base, where the supplementary angle α′ is the one adjacent to the perpendicular.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Left panel: acute-case triangle drawn in solid black outline with vertices α (lower left), γ (lower right), β (apex above the interior of the base)",
    "Horizontal base segment from α to γ labeled b, centered below the segment",
    "Left side from α up to β labeled c, placed outside the side near its midpoint",
    "Right side sloping from β down to γ labeled a, placed above/outside the side",
    "Dashed vertical altitude from β to a foot point on segment b, labeled h to the right of the dashed line",
    "Small square right-angle marker at the foot of the altitude, opening toward α",
    "Single arc at vertex α marking the interior angle, with the Greek label α just outside",
    "Vertex labels β (above apex), α (left of lower-left vertex), γ (right of lower-right vertex) in italic serif",
    "Right panel: obtuse-case triangle with the same lettering, apex β shifted left so it lies beyond α and the interior angle at α is obtuse",
    "Dashed horizontal extension of the base running leftward from α to the foot of the altitude",
    "Dashed vertical altitude from β down to that exterior foot, labeled h on its left",
    "Small square right-angle marker where the exterior altitude meets the dashed extension",
    "Two arcs at the lower-left corner: the larger interior angle α between side c and base b, and immediately left of it the angle α′ between side c and the dashed base extension",
    "Labels α and α′ set beside their respective arcs",
    "White background, no axes, no legend, all strokes thin black, auxiliary construction lines dashed"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "apexPosition",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Horizontal position of apex β",
      "range": [-0.6, 1.0, 0.02],
      "default": 0.35,
      "teaches": "That the altitude foot slides continuously along the base line as β moves, crossing α at the exact moment the angle at α becomes right and then obtuse, so the altitude is the same construction in every case — only its landing point changes."
    },
    {
      "id": "caseView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Case",
      "options": ["Acute at α", "Obtuse at α", "Side by side"],
      "default": "Acute at α",
      "teaches": "That the dashed base extension and the angle α′ are needed only in the obtuse case, and that α′ is the angle actually adjacent to the perpendicular there."
    },
    {
      "id": "buildAltitude",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "That clicking the base b drops the perpendicular from β and reveals the right-angle marker, turning the oblique triangle into a right triangle whose leg h is the height used in area = ½·b·h."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "An oblique triangle",
      "narration": "A triangle with base b from α to γ and apex β — no right angle anywhere. To find its area we still need a height.",
      "state": { "caseView": "Acute at α", "apexPosition": 0.35 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Drop the altitude",
      "narration": "Click on base b: a dashed perpendicular h falls from β and a small square marks the right angle. The oblique triangle now contains a right triangle with legs h and part of b.",
      "state": { "caseView": "Acute at α", "apexPosition": 0.35 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Slide β toward α",
      "narration": "Drag the apex left. The foot of the altitude travels along the base toward α while h stays perpendicular to the base line.",
      "state": { "caseView": "Acute at α", "apexPosition": 0.05 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Past α: the foot leaves the triangle",
      "narration": "When β passes to the left of α, the angle at α becomes obtuse and the altitude foot lands off the base — the base line must be extended by a dashed segment to catch it.",
      "state": { "caseView": "Obtuse at α", "apexPosition": -0.35 }
    },
    {
      "title": "The angle α′ adjacent to the perpendicular",
      "narration": "In the obtuse case two arcs appear: the interior angle α and its supplement α′ between c and the extension. It is α′ that sits in the right triangle, and sin α′ = sin α, so h = c·sin α either way.",
      "state": { "caseView": "Obtuse at α", "apexPosition": -0.35 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare both cases",
      "narration": "Seen side by side, the two constructions are the same move: drop a perpendicular from β to the line containing b. Only whether the foot lands inside or outside the triangle differs.",
      "state": { "caseView": "Side by side", "apexPosition": -0.35 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The height of a triangle is measured to the line containing the base, not to the base segment itself — so h = c·sin α works for obtuse triangles too, because the exterior angle α′ that actually forms the right triangle has exactly the same sine as α.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two clean line-art triangles on white, side by side, both wider than tall with the base horizontal. Left triangle: apex β roughly one-third of the way along the base and well above it; sides c (short, left) and a (long, right) labeled outside their midpoints; base labeled b below center; a vertical dashed altitude h from β with a small open square right-angle marker at its foot on b; a single small arc at α. Right triangle: identical lettering but the apex β sits left of and above α, so the left side c leans back to the right and the interior angle at α is clearly obtuse; the base is continued leftward with a dashed horizontal segment reaching under β, and the dashed altitude h drops from β onto that extension with a right-angle square at the foot; two adjacent arcs at the lower-left corner label α (interior, larger, opening right) and α′ (between c and the dashed extension, opening left). All geometry in thin black stroke; construction lines (altitude, base extension) dashed; vertex and side labels in italic serif, Greek letters for vertices, lowercase a, b, c, h for sides and height. When an interactive case is active, highlight the current right triangle (h, base-segment, and c) with a light accent fill so the learner sees the embedded right triangle emerge; keep everything else black-on-white."
}

